<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Mid-ordinate and trapezoidal estimates of a plot</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>{@nm@} wants to estimate the area of a plot near [[if test="is(ix=1)"]]Nyeri[[elif test="is(ix=2)"]]Kisii[[elif test="is(ix=3)"]]Embu[[elif test="is(ix=4)"]]Eldoret[[elif test="is(ix=5)"]]Kitale[[elif test="is(ix=6)"]]Meru[[elif test="is(ix=7)"]]Bungoma[[elif test="is(ix=8)"]]Kericho[[elif test="is(ix=9)"]]Murang'a[[else]]Nanyuki[[/if]]. The plot lies between a straight road and a curved stream. Starting at one corner, {@pr@} measured the width of the plot (from the road to the stream) every \({@g@}\,\text{m}\) along the road, which is \({@Lr@}\,\text{m}\) long.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Distance along road (m)</th>[[foreach u="xs"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@u@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Width (m)</th>[[foreach u="wl"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@u@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr></table><p>(a) Use the mid-ordinate rule with \({@n@}\) strips, each \({@hh@}\,\text{m}\) wide, to estimate the area of the plot.</p><div>[[input:ans1]] \(\text{m}^2\) [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Use the trapezoidal rule with the same \({@n@}\) strips to estimate the area.</p><div>[[input:ans2]] \(\text{m}^2\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) The land is valued at Ksh \({@p@}\) per square metre. By how much do the two estimates of its value differ?</p><div>Ksh [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The \({@n@}\) strips are \({@hh@}\,\text{m}\) wide, so their edges are at \(0, {@hh@}, {@2*hh@}, \ldots, {@Lr@}\,\text{m}\) and their middles at \({@g@}, {@3*g@}, {@5*g@}, \ldots\,\text{m}\).</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Distance (m)</th>[[foreach u="xs"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@u@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Width (m)</th>[[foreach u="wl"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@u@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Used as</th>[[foreach u="xs"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(mod(u,hh)=0)"]]edge[[else]]middle[[/if]]</td>[[/foreach]]</tr></table><p>(a) \(M = {@hh@} \times ({@apply("+",wmid)@}) = {@Ma@}\,\text{m}^2\), using only the widths at the middles.</p><p>(b) \(T = \tfrac{1}{2} \times {@hh@} \times \left[{@first(wedge)@} + {@last(wedge)@} + 2 \times {@apply("+",rest(rest(wedge),-1))@}\right] = {@Ta@}\,\text{m}^2\), using only the widths at the edges.</p><p>(c) The values are Ksh \({@MVd@}\) and Ksh \({@TVd@}\), so they differ by Ksh \({@Dd@}\) (that is, \({@Ddiff@}\,\text{m}^2 \times {@p@}\)).</p><p>The edge of the plot bends downwards, like an arch, so the trapezoidal estimate is too small and the mid-ordinate estimate too big: the true area lies between them.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
